# Importing a unit that declares `Text = class` must NOT change what `Text`
# means inside a DIFFERENT, later-compiled unit that never names it.
#
# It did, and it did so SILENTLY: SizeOf went 4128 (the RTL file record) -> 8
# (a class pointer) and the program still compiled. The trigger was import
# ORDER, and the cause was not the class-visibility machinery, which returned
# the correct verdict throughout.
#
# ParsingClassBodyCi ("no class scope is open" = -1) was initialised only in
# ParseProgram, the PASCAL entry point. Every other frontend started with the
# BSS default 0 -- a VALID class index -- so the compiler believed it was
# inside class 0's body for the whole compilation, and AddClassLikeType duly
# registered every top-level class of every imported unit as a NESTED type of
# it. ParseTypeKind consults FindNestedType BEFORE IsClassType, and class-scope
# lookup is deliberately not visibility-gated, so `Text` resolved to whichever
# unit got there first.
#
# The Pascal control (`uses` in both orders) passed throughout and is not a
# regression risk; this file is the arm that was broken.
# bug-nilpy-import-order-leaks-a-class-name-into-a-later-compiled-rtl-unit
import 'importordertextcls.pas'
import 'importordertextuse.pas'

ShowTextSize()
